📌 Algorithmic Time & Space Complexity
Time Complexity: O(NlogN)

The most computationally expensive step is sorting the list of events chronologically using events.sort(key=get_event_date), which takes O(NlogN) time, where N is the total number of events.

The single-pass loop iterating through the sorted list processes each event in O(1) constant time.

Space Complexity: O(U+M)

Storing the active user sessions requires space proportional to the number of unique machines (M) and active logged-in users (U) stored inside the set objects.

💡 Key Edge Cases Handled
Out-of-Order Log Ingestion: Log entries in real-world systems rarely arrive in strict chronological order due to network latency. Sorting by timestamp upfront guarantees that session histories are processed correctly regardless of input sequence.

Missing Login Logs (Orphaned Logouts): Using set.discard(user) instead of set.remove(user) prevents KeyError exceptions when processing a logout event for a user whose corresponding login was missing from the log window.

Duplicate Login Events: Using Python set collections instead of list ensures user names remain unique per machine even if multiple login events for the same user occur consecutively.

🚀 Suggested Enhancements for Scalability
Dictionary Default Handling: Replace standard dict initialization logic with collections.defaultdict(set) to eliminate explicit key-existence checks (if event.machine not in machines).

Timestamp Parsing: Upgrade the date attribute from plain string comparison to standard datetime.datetime objects to support flexible time formatting and cross-day delta calculations.

Stream Processing: For real-time monitoring, replace full-list sorting with a priority queue or time-series database stream to process events dynamically as they arrive.
